{
  "dom_id": "step-q115265",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(1,2)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0,2).$ If ${f}^{\u2032}(1)=-2,$ estimate the value of $f(0.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(1,2),$ so we have $f(1)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{\u2032}(1)=-2,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(-2)(x-1) \\\\  & =2-2(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.8) & \u2248L(0.8) \\\\  & =2-2(0.8-1) \\\\  & =2-2(-0.2) \\\\  & =2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(0,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph close to $x=1,$ and therefore we have an underestimate.",
